The recursive function checks the first and last characters, then calls itself for the smaller part of the string. It stops when the pointers meet or cross, which means all pairs matched.

A mismatch returns `false` right away. The empty string is also handled because the first call has its left pointer past the right pointer.

```java
class Solution {
    public boolean palindromeCheck(String s) {
        return checkFromEnds(s, 0, s.length() - 1);
    }

    private boolean checkFromEnds(String s, int left, int right) {
        if (left >= right) {
            return true;
        }

        if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
            return false;
        }

        return checkFromEnds(s, left + 1, right - 1);
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` because of the recursive calls.

Here the characters are pushed into a stack first. Reading them back gives the string in reverse order, so comparing that order with the original characters tells whether the string reads the same both ways.

The early comparison is useful for strings such as `"abca"`, where the first popped character is `a` but the first original character is `a`, and the next pair exposes the mismatch later. Empty input and a single character are safe because there is nothing to disagree with.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        bool palindromeCheck(string& s){
            stack<char> characters;

            for (char ch : s) {
                characters.push(ch);
            }

            for (char ch : s) {
                if (characters.top() != ch) {
                    return false;
                }
                characters.pop();
            }

            return true;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack.

Instead of making another string, this version checks pairs while moving outward from the center. Odd length strings have one center, while even length strings have a gap between two centers.

For `"abba"`, the starting pair is the two `b` characters. The next pair is `a` and `a`, so the whole string passes. A mismatch during expansion returns `false`.

```java
class Solution {   
    public boolean palindromeCheck(String s) {
        int left;
        int right;

        if (s.length() % 2 == 0) {
            left = s.length() / 2 - 1;
            right = s.length() / 2;
        } else {
            left = s.length() / 2 - 1;
            right = s.length() / 2 + 1;
        }

        while (left >= 0 && right < s.length()) {
            if (s.charAt(left) != s.charAt(right)) {
                return false;
            }
            --left;
            ++right;
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The original string stays unchanged.

Comparing matching characters from both ends keeps the check small. The left pointer moves right and the right pointer moves left. Any different pair means the string is not a palindrome.

For `"level"`, the comparisons are `l` with `l`, then `e` with `e`. The middle `v` does not need a partner. An empty string and a one-character string both return `true`.

```cpp
class Solution{
    public:
        bool palindromeCheck(string& s){
            int left = 0;
            int right = static_cast<int>(s.size()) - 1;

            while (left < right) {
                if (s[left] != s[right]) {
                    return false;
                }
                ++left;
                --right;
            }

            return true;
        }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`. The input string is not changed.

