## Approach
We use two pointers:

- `left` starts from the beginning of the string.
- `right` starts from the end of the string.

At each recursive call:

1. If `left >= right`, all character pairs have been checked, so the string is a palindrome.
2. If `s[left] != s[right]`, the string is not a palindrome.
3. Otherwise, move both pointers inward and recurse.

## Solution
```cpp[]
class Solution{	
    private:
        bool palindrome_string(string& s,int left, int right){
            if (left >= right) return true;
            // "We've successfully checked every pair of characters. The string is a palindrome."

            if(s[left] != s[right]) return false;

            return palindrome_string(s, left + 1, right - 1);
        }
	public:		
		bool palindromeCheck(string& s){
			return palindrome_string(s,0,s.length() - 1);
		}
};
```

